<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beagle Channel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Beagle Channel is a strait in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a> archipelago at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> over the islands at its eastern end was disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and brought them close to war in 1978. Papal mediation averted the conflict and led to the Treaty of Peace and Friendship of 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beagle-channel.jpg" alt="Beagle Channel" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ted Wakabayashi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:090111-6_Above_%E3%83%93%E3%83%BC%E3%82%B0%E3%83%AB%E6%B0%B4%E9%81%93.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Beagle Channel, called Onašaga in Yahgan, separates the main island of Isla Grande de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a> from smaller islands including Picton, Lennox, Nueva, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Navarino</a>, Hoste, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Londonderry</a> and Stewart. It is about 240 kilometres long and 5 kilometres wide at its narrowest point, and runs from Nueva Island in the east to Darwin Sound and Cook Bay in the Pacific in the west. About 50 kilometres from its western end it divides into two branches north and south of Gordon Island.</p><p>Its eastern part forms part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, and the western part lies wholly within Chile. With the Strait of Magellan and the Drake Passage, it is one of three navigable passages around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> between the Pacific and the Atlantic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Yaghan peoples settled the islands along the Murray Channel, which joins the southern side of the Beagle Channel, about 10,000 years ago. Bahia Wulaia on Isla Navarino, site of the Bahia Wulaia Dome Middens, is among the notable archaeological sites. In a Selkʼnam myth the channel was created, with the Strait of Magellan and Fagnano Lake, where slingshots fell to earth in Taiyín's fight with a witch.</p><p>The channel was named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a> during its first survey of the coasts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, from 1826 to 1830, under Commander Phillip Parker King. Captain Pringle Stokes died during that voyage and was replaced by Robert FitzRoy, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a> on the second voyage. Darwin first saw glaciers when the ship reached the channel on 29 January 1833. The painter Conrad Martens made watercolours there in 1833 and 1834.</p><p>Picton, Lennox and Nueva were the subject of the long-running Beagle conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. Incidents between the two navies occurred from the 1950s to the 1970s, among them the 1958 Snipe incident and the 1967 Cruz del Sur incident. Under the Treaty of Peace and Friendship of 1984 the islands are part of Chile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The largest settlement on the channel is Ushuaia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, followed by Puerto Williams in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, and both are among the southernmost settlements in the world. Under the 1984 treaty, ships of other nations navigate between the Strait of Magellan and Ushuaia with a Chilean pilot. The channel is a place to watch dolphins and porpoises, among them Peale's dolphin, which is endemic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beagle Channel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the twentieth century the armed forces intervened in politics in 1924–1925 and again in the coup of 1973, and a near war with Argentina over the <b>Beagle Channel</b> in 1978 was averted by papal mediation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Military</a></li></ul></section>
